+# clean-runs/lu-pair-single-core-20261008/. The original is untouched. It keeps the original's file name ON PURPOSE:
+# every harness's "is another session using the board?" guard looks for run_(parsec|pair)_session.exp in the
+# process list, so a copy under another name would be invisible to the older scripts and they could pkill its
+# picocom mid-run. Diff against the pristine copy: diff run_parsec_session.exp.orig run_parsec_session.exp
+#
+# What differs from the original (everything else, including the staging and every board step up to the measured
+# command, is byte-for-byte the sweep's, so a pair window has exactly the sweep's history behind it):
+#   1. LIB points at the ORIGINAL lib/ by absolute path (the copy does not sit next to it).
+#   2. -plan lupair: ONE point that runs TWO lu_ncb processes at the same time on the one core - same -n, two block
+#      sizes (-pair-n N, -pair-b BA,BB). Both are started in the background of the one measured sh -c and the window
+#      closes when BOTH have exited (run to completion; there is no per-program instruction counter to cut them at the
+#      same work). Each child's stdout/stderr go to a.out/a.err and b.out/b.err; their exit codes are echoed as
+#      "PAIR-RC a=<rc> b=<rc>" and the shell's exit status is 0 only if both are 0.
+#   3. After a measured window, post_window prints the two reports (cat a.out b.out a.err b.err) as a SEPARATE command,
+#      outside the counter window. Only for -plan lupair.
+# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
